Q: HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3?

Very common backend interview comparison.
Remember: these are **HTTP versions**. HTTPS = any of them + TLS (HTTP/3 encryption is built into QUIC).

Also: “2” ≠ two-way. See `HTTP versions vs HTTPS`.

────────────────────────────────────────
Quick table

| Feature               | HTTP/1.1                 | HTTP/2                         | HTTP/3                    |
| --------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Year                  | 1997                     | 2015                           | ~2022                     |
| Format                | Text                     | Binary frames                  | Binary                    |
| Transport             | TCP                      | TCP                            | QUIC over UDP             |
| Multiplexing          | No (practical)           | Yes (streams on 1 TCP conn)    | Yes (streams on QUIC)     |
| Head-of-line blocking | App-level HoL            | TCP-level HoL still possible   | Stream HoL largely solved |
| Header compression    | No                       | HPACK                          | QPACK                     |
| Connections           | Many TCP conns (browsers)| Usually one TCP connection     | One QUIC connection       |
| Performance           | Slowest for many assets  | Faster                         | Best on lossy/mobile      |

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ HTTP/1.1

- Text protocol
- Browsers open ~6–8 TCP connections per host
- One connection handles requests mostly one-after-another

HoL example on one connection:
Request1 waiting (slow)
Request2 waiting
Request3 waiting

Workarounds (old web): domain sharding, sprites, many connections — still limited.

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ HTTP/2

Improvements:
1) Multiplexing — many streams on **one TCP** connection
2) Binary frames — faster parsing
3) HPACK header compression
4) Server Push (optional; less used now)

Still on TCP → if one TCP packet is lost, TCP holds later data until retransmit
→ **TCP-level head-of-line blocking** can delay all streams.

Stream1 packet lost ❌
Stream2 waiting
Stream3 waiting

────────────────────────────────────────
3️⃣ HTTP/3

Big change: **QUIC over UDP** (not raw plain UDP app protocol).

QUIC provides:
- multiplexing
- encryption (TLS integrated)
- reliability **per stream**
- faster setup (TLS+transport combined; 0-RTT possible)

Lost packet in one stream:
Stream1 lost → retransmit that stream only
Stream2 continues ✅
Stream3 continues ✅

Better on mobile / Wi-Fi↔cellular switches.

Stack:
HTTP/1.1 → TCP → IP  
HTTP/2   → TCP → IP  
HTTP/3   → QUIC → UDP → IP  

────────────────────────────────────────
Head-of-Line Blocking (senior follow-up)

Definition:
A delay/loss at the “front” blocks work behind it.

| Protocol | HoL? | Notes |
| -------- | ---- | ----- |
| HTTP/1.1 | Yes (request queue on connection) | Multiple connections workaround |
| HTTP/2   | Yes at TCP layer if packet loss | Multiplexing helps until loss |
| HTTP/3   | Effectively solved at stream layer | Independent QUIC streams |

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ HTTP/3 uses UDP — do we lose data?

UDP alone is unreliable.
HTTP/3 does **not** mean “fire and forget like raw UDP.”

QUIC adds reliability on top of UDP:
- detect loss
- retransmit
- order per stream

So interview line:
“HTTP/3 uses UDP for transport flexibility, but QUIC makes delivery reliable — data isn’t just dropped silently like plain UDP.”

────────────────────────────────────────
Website loading example

Assets: HTML, CSS, JS, images

HTTP/1.1 → limited parallel TCP connections → waiting  
HTTP/2 → many streams, one TCP → faster (unless packet loss)  
HTTP/3 → independent streams + better loss recovery → often best on bad networks  

Modern sites (Google/Cloudflare/etc.) often support HTTP/2 and HTTP/3.

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring Boot note

Local APIs often HTTP/1.1.
You can enable HTTP/2 in Spring (usually with TLS):
server.http2.enabled=true

HTTP/3 support depends on server/proxy (often terminated at Nginx/Cloudflare/load balancer).

────────────────────────────────────────
Best short interview answer

“HTTP/1.1 is text-based and needs many connections with request HoL issues.
HTTP/2 is binary, multiplexes streams on one TCP connection with header compression, but TCP packet loss can still block all streams.
HTTP/3 runs over QUIC/UDP with independent reliable streams, reducing HoL blocking and improving mobile performance.”
